<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What Android's automatic backup carries, on Android 11 and below.

  THIS IS THE APP'S REAL BACKUP, and it costs no code, no permission, no
  sign-in and no OAuth client. Android copies the app's private storage to the
  user's Google account roughly once a day while the phone is idle, charging
  and on wi-fi, then restores it when they set up a new phone or reinstall.
  It is the mechanism people already understand from their chat app, and the
  farmer never has to know it is happening.

  The ledger is a SQLite file under databases/, which is included by default.
  It is listed explicitly anyway: a default nobody wrote down is a default
  somebody removes by accident, and this file is the entire farm.

  ONLY THESE DOMAINS EXIST: root, file, database, sharedpref, external, and
  their device_ prefixed twins. There is no `cache` domain — an earlier
  version of this file tried to exclude one and failed the build. Cache
  directories are excluded by Android automatically and cannot be opted back
  in, so there is nothing to say about them here.

  NAMING AN <include> MAKES IT AN ALLOW-LIST. Everything not listed is left
  out, which is why there are no <exclude> rules here at all: shared
  preferences, caches and external storage are already excluded by not being
  mentioned. Lint enforces this — an <exclude> whose domain is not also
  included is a fatal error, and it cost a build to learn.

  That omission is deliberate rather than lazy. CapacitorStorage holds the
  chosen language and other WebView scratch state; restoring it onto different
  hardware is the kind of thing that produces a bug nobody can reproduce, and
  the farmer re-picking Kannada once is cheaper.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <include domain="database" path="." />
    <include domain="file" path="." />
</full-backup-content>
